Counterexample to Thm 3.2(d) / Summary item 2 without the hypothesis tau < sigma:
  first random instance: point (alpha, rho) = (5, 253/18) has zero-window left steepness 25/18
  and right flatness 25/18 >= 25/18, so it is a zero-window (25/18, 25/18)-kink, but it is not a vertex.
  minimal instance: points [(0, '10'), (1, '5'), (2, '0')]; vertices [(0, '10'), (2, '0')];
  (1, 5) is a zero-window (5, 5)-kink (left secant 5, right secant 5) but lies inside the edge.
  With tau < sigma required (as the proof uses), the forward direction holds in all tested cases.

Strictness in Thm 3.2(a)/(b): at lambda = sigma (resp. tau) exactly, a lambda-MAP can sit outside
  the window: observed 3494 times (left) and 3357 times (right); the theorem's strict inequalities are needed.
